<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monte Cassino</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 529</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Abbey of Monte Cassino</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monte Cassino is a hill above Cassino in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-of-nursia">Benedict of Nursia</a> founded his monastery around 529, from which the Benedictine order grew. Sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> around 577 and by Arab raiders in 883, it was rebuilt and became a great centre of learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> copying, closely linked with the Lombard principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> and Capua. The abbey was destroyed by Allied bombing in 1944 and rebuilt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monte-cassino.jpg" alt="Monte Cassino" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Monticiano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MonteCassino_Abbey.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The abbey stands on a rocky hill about 130 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in the Latin Valley, on the site of the ancient Roman town of Casinum. The Volsci first settled the nearby town of Cassino in the fifth century BCE and built a citadel on the summit. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeated them in 312 BCE and built a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> there. Casinum became a bishopric in the fifth century CE, but it lacked strong defences and was later abandoned and neglected. The hill overlooks Highway 6 and has always been of strategic importance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a>'s Life of Saint Benedict, Benedict smashed the idol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, overturned the altar and dedicated one chapel to Saint Martin and another to Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>. Excavations after 1944 found traces of both oratories. At Monte Cassino Benedict wrote the Rule that became the foundation of Western monasticism and received a visit from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a>, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, and he died there. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> sacked the first monastery, the monks fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Petronax of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> re-established it in 718.</p><p>In 744 a donation from Gisulf II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> created the Terra Sancti Benedicti, secular lands answerable only to the abbot and the pope. Saracens sacked and burned the abbey in the ninth century, and the monks lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum">Teano</a> and then Capua until it was rebuilt in 949.</p><p>The abbey reached its height in the 11th century under Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desiderius">Desiderius</a>, later Pope Victor III. He brought Byzantine mosaicists from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and the abbey church was consecrated by Pope Alexander II in 1071. The library, the scriptorium and the school of illuminators became widely known, and the Beneventan script flourished. Three popes came from the abbey: Stephen IX, Victor III and Gelasius II. An earthquake damaged it in 1349, French troops sacked it in 1799, and the Italian state dissolved it in 1866 and made it a national monument.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>During the Battle of Monte Cassino in 1944 the abbey lay on the German Gustav Line. On 15 February 1944 Allied air raids nearly destroyed it, on reports that German troops occupied it. None were present, and the only people killed in the monastery were 230 Italian civilians sheltering there. German paratroopers then occupied the ruins. The abbey was rebuilt after the war with the support of President Luigi Einaudi, and Pope Paul VI consecrated the rebuilt basilica on 24 October 1964. It remains a Benedictine monastery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Monte Cassino” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The duchy developed the Beneventan script and was a cultural centre linked to <b>Monte Cassino</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-benevento">Duchy of Benevento</a></li><li><blockquote>Its judges ruled the largest of the four Sardinian realms, granting land and churches to Italian and French monasteries such as <b>Montecassino</b> and Saint-Victor of Marseille.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-cagliari">Judicate of Cagliari</a></li><li><blockquote>Pandulf IV, called the Wolf of the Abruzzi, despoiled <b>Monte Cassino</b> and, like his rivals, hired Norman mercenaries, helping the Normans gain a foothold in Campania.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-capua">Principality of Capua</a></li></ul></section>
